# Development Lifecycle Standards

## Code Documentation
- Every module must have a top-level docstring describing its purpose, author, and last modified date
- Every class must have a docstring explaining its responsibility
- Every function/method must have a Google-style docstring with Args, Returns, and Raises sections
- Inline comments required for any logic block longer than 5 lines
- Complex algorithms must include a plain-English explanation comment above the block

## File Header Template
Every .py file must begin with:
```python
"""
Module: <module_name>
Purpose: <one line description>
Author: Nathan
Created: <date>
Last Modified: <date>

Description:
    <multi-line detailed description of what this module does>
"""
```

## Structure Rules
- No function longer than 50 lines — refactor into helpers if exceeded
- No file longer than 400 lines — split into submodules if exceeded
- Group imports: stdlib → third-party → local, separated by blank lines
- Constants defined in config/constants.py, never hardcoded inline
- All user-facing strings defined in config/constants.py as STRING constants (for future i18n support)

## Testing
- Every core/ module must have a corresponding test file in tests/
- Tests must cover: happy path, empty input, error/exception cases
- Use pytest fixtures for reusable test data
- Run tests before every GitHub push

## Changelog
- Maintain a CHANGELOG.md at root level
- Format: [version] - [date] - [Added / Changed / Fixed / Removed]
- Update on every meaningful feature addition or bug fix

## Error Handling
- Never use bare except: clauses — always catch specific exceptions
- All caught exceptions must be logged via the app logger
- User-facing errors must show human-readable messages, not tracebacks
- Critical errors (file system failures) must write to logs/ immediately
